{"type":"WebSearch","query":"California Proposition 3 2026 election results November 3","content":"**Unofficial results for California Proposition 3 in the November 3, 2026, general election are not yet available.** The official California Secretary of State election results site currently shows 0 precincts reporting and 0 votes counted (as of late November 2). Polls close at 8 p.m. PT on election night, after which live unofficial results will begin appearing; certified results are expected by December 11, 2026. Check the official site for updates: electionresults.sos.ca.gov.[[1]](https://electionresults.sos.ca.gov/returns/ballot-measures/county)

Proposition 3 is an initiated constitutional amendment titled “Provides Permanent Funding for Schools and Health Care by Extending Existing Tax on High Incomes.” It would make permanent the higher state income tax rates on high earners (originally from 2012’s Prop 30 and extended in 2016) that are otherwise set to expire after 2030. These rates (10.3%–12.3%, plus a 1% surcharge over $1 million) apply to income above roughly $371,000 for single filers or $742,000 for joint filers (2025 levels, inflation-adjusted). Revenue (estimated $5–15 billion annually, varying with the economy) would go 89% to K–12 schools and 11% to community colleges. A yes vote makes the tax permanent; a no vote lets it expire.[[2]](https://lao.ca.gov/BallotAnalysis/Proposition?number=3&year=2026)

Pre-election polling (e.g., PPIC and UC Berkeley IGS) showed majority support in the mid-to-high 50s percent range, with forecasts indicating it was very likely to pass. Supporters included the California Teachers Association; opponents included taxpayer groups. Turnout and final counts (including mail and provisional ballots processed after election night) will determine the outcome. For the latest, visit the Secretary of State results page or news outlets tracking the 14 statewide propositions.","citations":["https://electionresults.sos.ca.gov/returns/ballot-measures/county","https://lao.ca.gov/BallotAnalysis/Proposition?number=3&year=2026"],"allowed_domains":null}

{"type":"WebSearch","query":"PPIC poll Proposition 3 October 2026 yes no","content":"**The October 2026 PPIC Statewide Survey (fielded October 16–22 among 1,086 likely voters) found 58% yes and 41% no on Proposition 3 (1% don’t know/undecided), with a ±3.8% margin of error.**[[1]](https://www.ppic.org/wp-content/uploads/crosstabs-likely-voters-1026.pdf)

Proposition 3 would make permanent the existing voter-approved higher income tax rates on individuals earning over $371,000 (adjusted for inflation) to fund schools and health care. Support was similar to PPIC’s September 2026 poll (58% yes, 40% no). It was strongest among Democrats (around 80%), independents, younger voters, renters, and lower-income groups, with majorities in most regions (Central Valley more divided). Republicans largely opposed it.[[2]](https://www.ppic.org/publication/ppic-statewide-survey-californians-and-their-government-october-2026/)

This is from PPIC’s *Californians and Their Government* survey; full crosstabs and methodology are available on ppic.org. Other polls (e.g., Berkeley IGS) showed slightly lower yes support around the same time.","citations":["https://www.ppic.org/wp-content/uploads/crosstabs-likely-voters-1026.pdf","https://www.ppic.org/publication/ppic-statewide-survey-californians-and-their-government-october-2026/"],"allowed_domains":null}

{"type":"WebSearch","query":"California Proposition 30 2012 Proposition 55 2016 election results percentage","content":"**California Proposition 30 (2012):** Approved. Yes: 55.37% (7,014,114 votes); No: 44.63% (5,653,636 votes). Total votes: 12,667,750.[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_California_Proposition_30)

**California Proposition 55 (2016):** Approved. Yes: 63.27% (8,594,273 votes); No: 36.73% (4,988,329 votes). Valid votes: 13,582,602.[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_California_Proposition_55)

These are the certified statewide results. Proposition 30 temporarily raised income taxes on high earners and the sales tax to fund education (the sales tax portion later expired). Proposition 55 extended the income tax increases through 2030, directing funds to education and healthcare.","citations":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2012_California_Proposition_30","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_California_Proposition_55"],"allowed_domains":null}

{"type":"WebSearch","query":"California Proposition 3 2026 prediction market Kalshi "Yes" price November","content":"**Kalshi "Yes" price for California Proposition 3 (2026) in November: ~94.5¢ (94.5% implied probability of passing), as of Nov. 2, 2026 (evening ET).** Bid/ask was 93.0%/96.0%, with ~2.6K contracts traded.[[1]](https://midterms.nijinn.io/ballot-measures/california-proposition-3-high-earner-permanent-tax/)

This is for the market on whether Prop 3 (making the existing high-earner income tax rates permanent to fund schools and health care, instead of letting them expire in 2031) passes in the Nov. 3, 2026 election. Earlier November/October data showed it in the high 80s to mid-90s (e.g., ~89% in mid-October, ~94-95% by late October/early November).[[2]](https://defirate.com/odds/california-props/)

Prices are live and can move; check Kalshi directly (related markets under California ballot measures) for the latest, as this is a thin market. Aggregators like Nijinn and PredictionScout tracked it similarly around 94% on Nov. 2. Polymarket was a bit lower (~90.5%). The measure has polled in the mid-to-high 50s yes among likely voters.","citations":["https://midterms.nijinn.io/ballot-measures/california-proposition-3-high-earner-permanent-tax/","https://defirate.com/odds/california-props/"],"allowed_domains":null}

